1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,219
In January 2022, a dozen eggs in America cost

2
00:00:04,219 --> 00:00:08,137
one dollar and ninety-three cents on average.

3
00:00:08,137 --> 00:00:14,233
By March 2025, the same dozen cost six dollars and twenty-three cents.

4
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:17,020
The highest price ever recorded.

5
00:00:17,470 --> 00:00:18,298
Then it fell.

6
00:00:18,298 --> 00:00:23,390
In October 2026, the average dozen was back to two dollars and thirty-two cents.

7
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:27,317
The official explanation was simple: bird flu.

8
00:00:27,317 --> 00:00:28,980
And bird flu was real.

9
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:31,248
Tens of millions of hens died.

10
00:00:31,248 --> 00:00:33,440
But it's not the whole story.

11
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:39,463
In June 2026, the Justice Department and seventeen states accused three of the

12
00:00:39,463 --> 00:00:42,535
biggest egg companies in America of pushing

13
00:00:42,535 --> 00:00:45,607
up a price that almost nobody has heard of.

14
00:00:45,607 --> 00:00:48,250
The companies denied it, and settled.

15
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:53,839
So when you paid six dollars for a dozen eggs, who actually got the money?

16
00:00:53,839 --> 00:00:58,700
To find out, let's follow one dozen eggs, from the hen to your fridge.

17
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:06,748
Our dozen starts in a barn with a few hundred thousand hens.

18
00:01:06,748 --> 00:01:10,677
America has around three hundred million laying hens,

19
00:01:10,677 --> 00:01:13,420
and a good hen lays an egg most days.

20
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:17,823
There's a good chance our hen belongs to Cal-Maine Foods.

21
00:01:17,823 --> 00:01:20,181
It started in Mississippi in 1957.

22
00:01:20,181 --> 00:01:23,649
Today it's the biggest egg company in the country,

23
00:01:23,649 --> 00:01:26,423
with about thirteen billion eggs a year.

24
00:01:26,423 --> 00:01:30,030
That's about one in every five eggs sold in America.

25
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:33,380
Here's the first thing to know about eggs.

26
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:38,005
An egg from one big farm is almost the same as an egg from another.

27
00:01:38,005 --> 00:01:40,698
So farms don't really compete on brand.

28
00:01:40,698 --> 00:01:42,217
They compete on price.

29
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:44,840
And that price moves every single day.

30
00:01:45,290 --> 00:01:46,968
One more change matters.

31
00:01:46,968 --> 00:01:51,652
More and more hens live cage-free, almost half of them by mid 2025,

32
00:01:51,652 --> 00:01:55,846
because several states and many big retailers now demand it.

33
00:01:55,846 --> 00:01:58,363
Cage-free eggs cost more to produce.

34
00:01:58,363 --> 00:02:00,810
But they're not why prices tripled.

35
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:07,392
In early 2022, a deadly strain of bird flu reached American farms.

36
00:02:07,392 --> 00:02:08,721
It spreads fast.

37
00:02:08,721 --> 00:02:12,791
And when it's found in a barn, the rule is harsh:

38
00:02:12,791 --> 00:02:18,190
the whole flock is destroyed, sometimes millions of hens at once.

39
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:22,427
By April 2025, more than a hundred and sixty-eight

40
00:02:22,427 --> 00:02:26,592
million birds had been culled since the outbreak began.

41
00:02:26,592 --> 00:02:30,455
In the worst stretch, from late 2024 to early 2025,

42
00:02:30,455 --> 00:02:33,560
egg farms lost fifty million laying hens.

43
00:02:34,010 --> 00:02:37,160
Before the outbreak, America had more than

44
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,610
three hundred and fifteen million laying hens.

45
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:46,010
By March 2025, it was down to about two hundred and eighty-five million.

46
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:50,995
Now, a shortage of ten percent doesn't usually triple a price.

47
00:02:50,995 --> 00:02:52,532
But eggs are special.

48
00:02:52,532 --> 00:02:58,164
People keep buying them, for breakfast, for baking, in thousands of products.

49
00:02:58,164 --> 00:03:01,237
When supply drops and demand barely moves,

50
00:03:01,237 --> 00:03:05,260
the price has to jump a lot before anyone stops buying.

51
00:03:05,710 --> 00:03:09,732
On February twenty-eighth, 2025, a dozen large eggs sold

52
00:03:09,732 --> 00:03:14,256
wholesale in the Midwest for eight dollars and forty-one cents.

53
00:03:14,256 --> 00:03:17,990
A record, and more than three times the year before.

54
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:20,598
And we had seen this movie before.

55
00:03:20,598 --> 00:03:23,834
In January 2023, after the first wave of the virus,

56
00:03:23,834 --> 00:03:27,388
the average dozen hit four dollars and eighty-two cents.

57
00:03:27,388 --> 00:03:30,751
By August, it was back to two dollars and four cents.

58
00:03:30,751 --> 00:03:35,320
Then the virus came back, and the price climbed again, higher than ever.

59
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:38,008
So far, this is a supply chain story.

60
00:03:38,008 --> 00:03:40,730
But who decides what that wholesale price is?

61
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:44,093
Most eggs aren't sold at auction.

62
00:03:44,093 --> 00:03:49,784
Big buyers, like supermarket chains and food makers, sign contracts with egg farms.

63
00:03:49,784 --> 00:03:52,939
And many of those contracts don't fix a price.

64
00:03:52,939 --> 00:03:56,367
They point to a benchmark: a daily price published

65
00:03:56,367 --> 00:03:59,110
by a private company called Urner Barry.

66
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:01,529
Think of it like a thermostat.

67
00:04:01,529 --> 00:04:04,221
Whatever number Urner Barry prints today,

68
00:04:04,221 --> 00:04:07,240
millions of eggs in those contracts follow it.

69
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:10,034
So how does Urner Barry find its number?

70
00:04:10,034 --> 00:04:14,780
One of its inputs is an electronic trading platform called the Egg Clearinghouse,

71
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:18,589
or E C I, where producers and buyers trade spare eggs day by day.

72
00:04:18,589 --> 00:04:19,761
It's a small market.

73
00:04:19,761 --> 00:04:23,570
But what happens there helps set the price for a much bigger one.

74
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:25,923
That creates a strange incentive.

75
00:04:25,923 --> 00:04:29,845
If you sell a lot of eggs at the benchmark price, a higher number on

76
00:04:29,845 --> 00:04:33,940
that small platform can be worth a fortune on everything else you sell.

77
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:38,208
That's exactly what the government says happened.

78
00:04:38,208 --> 00:04:42,346
On June twenty-ninth, 2026, the Justice Department and seventeen

79
00:04:42,346 --> 00:04:45,837
states filed an antitrust complaint against Cal-Maine,

80
00:04:45,837 --> 00:04:50,880
Versova and Hickman's Egg Ranch, three of the country's largest egg producers.

81
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:55,201
The complaint alleges that from June 2022 to March 2025,

82
00:04:55,201 --> 00:05:00,178
right through the bird flu crisis, the three companies coordinated their

83
00:05:00,178 --> 00:05:04,810
bids on the Egg Clearinghouse, to push Urner Barry's quotes higher.

84
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:06,330
Here's the twist.

85
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:09,917
Some of them were, in the complaint's words, "net short".

86
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:14,070
They had promised supermarkets more eggs than their own hens laid.

87
00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:17,342
So they bought eggs on the platform to fill the gap.

88
00:05:17,342 --> 00:05:20,552
Bidding more for those eggs sounds like a bad deal.

89
00:05:20,552 --> 00:05:25,460
Unless it lifts the benchmark that sets the price on all your other contracts.

90
00:05:25,910 --> 00:05:28,932
New York's attorney general gave one example.

91
00:05:28,932 --> 00:05:33,902
In December 2022, Hickman's chief executive at the time emailed executives

92
00:05:33,902 --> 00:05:38,200
at Versova and Cal-Maine, urging "strong bids, early and often".

93
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:41,827
The three companies then placed dozens of higher bids,

94
00:05:41,827 --> 00:05:44,110
and Urner Barry raised its quotes.

95
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:48,689
In March 2025, the Justice Department opened its investigation.

96
00:05:48,689 --> 00:05:51,966
And according to the complaint, once the companies

97
00:05:51,966 --> 00:05:55,440
learned about it, the quotes "dropped significantly".

98
00:05:55,890 --> 00:05:57,753
The case never went to trial.

99
00:05:57,753 --> 00:06:02,571
The three companies settled for three point three million dollars in total,

100
00:06:02,571 --> 00:06:05,912
plus fifty-three million eggs donated to food banks.

101
00:06:05,912 --> 00:06:08,032
None of them admitted wrongdoing.

102
00:06:08,032 --> 00:06:11,372
Cal-Maine said its conduct was lawful, and its chief

103
00:06:11,372 --> 00:06:14,970
executive said the deal lets the company "move forward".

104
00:06:15,420 --> 00:06:20,576
To be clear, nobody has proved in court that these bids raised what you paid.

105
00:06:20,576 --> 00:06:22,451
Bird flu was the main shock.

106
00:06:22,451 --> 00:06:26,402
But the case shows how a few bids on a small platform could

107
00:06:26,402 --> 00:06:30,420
ripple through the price of a dozen eggs across the country.

108
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:34,216
So who made money during the shortage?

109
00:06:34,216 --> 00:06:36,273
Look at the biggest producer.

110
00:06:36,273 --> 00:06:38,472
In its fiscal year to May 2025,

111
00:06:38,472 --> 00:06:42,657
Cal-Maine's sales reached four point three billion dollars.

112
00:06:42,657 --> 00:06:45,920
Its profit: one point two two billion dollars.

113
00:06:46,370 --> 00:06:49,228
Bird flu destroyed hens all over the country.

114
00:06:49,228 --> 00:06:52,658
But when prices triple, the farms that still have hens

115
00:06:52,658 --> 00:06:55,770
can earn more than ever, even selling fewer eggs.

116
00:06:56,220 --> 00:06:56,994
Now compare.

117
00:06:56,994 --> 00:07:01,574
Cal-Maine's share of the settlement was one and a half million dollars.

118
00:07:01,574 --> 00:07:04,542
One law firm worked out that it was about zero

119
00:07:04,542 --> 00:07:07,380
point one two percent of that year's profit.

120
00:07:07,830 --> 00:07:12,198
In other words, for every thousand dollars Cal-Maine earned that year,

121
00:07:12,198 --> 00:07:15,630
the settlement cost it about a dollar and twenty cents.

122
00:07:16,730 --> 00:07:20,487
Then the cycle turned, as it always does in farming.

123
00:07:20,487 --> 00:07:24,099
High prices pulled new hens into barns everywhere.

124
00:07:24,099 --> 00:07:26,050
Farms rebuilt their flocks.

125
00:07:26,050 --> 00:07:28,579
Hundreds of small new farms opened.

126
00:07:28,579 --> 00:07:32,986
By the end of September 2026, America had about three hundred

127
00:07:32,986 --> 00:07:37,610
and eighteen million laying hens, more than before the outbreak.

128
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:40,578
The wholesale price collapsed.

129
00:07:40,578 --> 00:07:46,621
In January 2026, a dozen large eggs sold wholesale for thirty-six cents,

130
00:07:46,621 --> 00:07:50,230
down almost ninety percent in seven months.

131
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:53,755
By April, it was twenty-two cents a dozen,

132
00:07:53,755 --> 00:07:57,700
below what it costs most farms to produce them.

133
00:07:58,150 --> 00:08:00,939
The industry's own marketing group put it this way:

134
00:08:00,939 --> 00:08:03,510
"There's never been a better time to buy eggs."

135
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:06,909
For Cal-Maine, the boom became a bust.

136
00:08:06,909 --> 00:08:11,954
In its fiscal year to May 2026, profit fell seventy-four percent,

137
00:08:11,954 --> 00:08:15,601
to three hundred and seventeen million dollars.

138
00:08:15,601 --> 00:08:18,317
In the last quarter, it lost money.

139
00:08:18,317 --> 00:08:22,120
Its chief executive blamed "industry oversupply".

140
00:08:22,570 --> 00:08:26,332
So the company is trying to depend less on that wild daily price.

141
00:08:26,332 --> 00:08:30,327
It's spending fifty-four million dollars to make more prepared foods,

142
00:08:30,327 --> 00:08:34,610
like egg bites and breakfast products, where prices don't swing every day.

143
00:08:35,710 --> 00:08:37,589
Last stop: the supermarket.

144
00:08:37,589 --> 00:08:40,790
And this is where it gets interesting for you.

145
00:08:41,240 --> 00:08:45,809
Between the farm and your cart, our dozen passes through several hands.

146
00:08:45,809 --> 00:08:49,735
It's washed, graded and packed, often by the producer itself.

147
00:08:49,735 --> 00:08:54,176
Then it's trucked to a distribution center, and finally to the store.

148
00:08:54,176 --> 00:08:55,592
Each step takes a cut.

149
00:08:55,592 --> 00:08:59,582
When wholesale prices are high, the farm's cut is the biggest.

150
00:08:59,582 --> 00:09:02,800
When they crash, the farm's cut almost disappears.

151
00:09:03,250 --> 00:09:07,470
In April 2026, when farms were getting twenty-two cents a dozen,

152
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:11,690
the average advertised price in stores was a dollar sixty-eight.

153
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:14,920
That's more than seven times the wholesale price.

154
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:17,492
And the average shelf price that month,

155
00:09:17,492 --> 00:09:21,250
across all stores, was two dollars and twenty-five cents.

156
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:24,724
By late September, wholesale was thirty-nine cents.

157
00:09:24,724 --> 00:09:28,460
The average shelf price was still two dollars and twenty-seven.

158
00:09:28,910 --> 00:09:29,996
Put it another way.

159
00:09:29,996 --> 00:09:34,110
Out of the twelve eggs you paid for, the farm's price covered about two.

160
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:36,223
Part of that gap is normal.

161
00:09:36,223 --> 00:09:39,735
Eggs need washing, grading, packing, trucks, and a store.

162
00:09:39,735 --> 00:09:42,877
And many retailers buy on contracts that last three

163
00:09:42,877 --> 00:09:46,080
or six months, so lower prices reach the shelf late.

164
00:09:46,530 --> 00:09:48,586
To be fair, it works both ways.

165
00:09:48,586 --> 00:09:53,360
At the peak, some stores sold eggs at a loss to keep shoppers coming in.

166
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:58,930
But for most of the past year, the gap has been wide, and shoppers have paid for it.

167
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:02,788
So, who really got paid for six-dollar eggs?

168
00:10:02,788 --> 00:10:03,603
Not the hens.

169
00:10:03,603 --> 00:10:06,611
Not most small farms, which now sell below cost.

170
00:10:06,611 --> 00:10:11,688
The money went to whoever held eggs when they were scarce: the biggest producers,

171
00:10:11,688 --> 00:10:15,699
with one point two billion dollars of profit at Cal-Maine alone.

172
00:10:15,699 --> 00:10:19,899
And now that eggs are cheap again, most of your two dollars goes to

173
00:10:19,899 --> 00:10:23,910
everyone after the farm: the packers, the trucks, and the store.

174
00:10:24,360 --> 00:10:27,738
The deeper lesson is about how that price is made.

175
00:10:27,738 --> 00:10:31,252
A dozen eggs in your cart is priced off a benchmark,

176
00:10:31,252 --> 00:10:36,184
fed by a small trading platform, that most Americans have never heard of.

177
00:10:36,184 --> 00:10:39,360
When that number moves, the whole country pays.

178
00:10:39,810 --> 00:10:43,218
The government says three companies tried to move it.

179
00:10:43,218 --> 00:10:46,882
They paid three point three million dollars and gave away

180
00:10:46,882 --> 00:10:50,290
fifty-three million eggs, without admitting anything.

181
00:10:50,740 --> 00:10:54,645
Today a dozen costs about two dollars and thirty-two cents.

182
00:10:54,645 --> 00:10:59,675
And bird flu is still out there, eighteen million hens lost this year alone.

183
00:10:59,675 --> 00:11:03,183
Next time the price jumps, you'll know where to look.

184
00:11:03,183 --> 00:11:04,573
Not just at the barn.

185
00:11:04,573 --> 00:11:05,500
At the number.
